00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.880 align:start position:0%
 
you'll be lost for hours in the first  iteration of Dark Souls two's DLC and I  mean that literally because level design  is one of the most enticing parts of the  sunken crown DLC the entire zone feels  like a huge maze made up of smaller  mazes inside of it and that is a  wonderful thing for a sequel that  suffered from really poor linear level  design until now I was lost in the  sunken ground DLC and I loved it because  you see it's one thing to force your way  past hordes of enemies to get to the  boss at the end and it's another thing  completely to do that while trying to  maintain a sense of direction and a  sense of space the DLC forces you to  focus on your surroundings so you can  make your way through the level as  efficiently as possible so I cared about  the room with the spikes on the ground  and I cared about the staircase with the  walking poisoned statues of death I was  forced to care about my surroundings  because it helped me conquer the sunken  city to be lost is to be a must and I  felt like I was properly exploring for  the first time in Dark Souls 2 because  it wasn't just me against the enemies  you know it was me against the world so  at the level design is a really awesome  aspect of the DLC and as you can  probably see in the footage the world  can be used to your advantage there are  these blue switches that predictably  alter the geography of the level raising  pillars with hollows in the middle  giving you multiple interactions with  different levels of one elevator the  most satisfying way I found to use these  switches was with a bow because it  allows you to block off the line of  sight for arches in the distance or  maybe raising a pillar into a mob of  enemies and I honestly got this really  really cool Legend of Zelda vibe from  some of the rooms rooms which require  you to activate switches while standing  on different platforms movement and  platforming in this way is simple like  in the Legend of Zelda but it's  satisfying and it's incredibly effective  in a Souls game and more of this  geography altering and puzzle solving is  something I'd love to see in the souls  games it's pretty easy to dismiss some  of these enemies as simple but I thought  about it and you know each enemy had  something that made them unique in terms  of gameplay they all have one quirk that  makes them difficult to kill or they all  have some subtle way of  killing you for example the sunken  Knights challenged my established way of  playing with a strength build because  they never staggered some Knights will  have an aura of poison that punishes  your backstab attempts and the bogeys  throughout the level will break your  weapon forcing you into a backup weapon  that you're less familiar with and that  really added the feeling of adventure  going through the DLC I had to actually  change my weapon as I went through and  using a weapon that you're not familiar  with or having a backup weapon that you  have to resort to really adds to that  element of adventure you know you have  to actually use everything in your  arsenal to try and get through the level  and I'm glad they made it so that things  in this level really really wear down  the durability of your gear my favourite  enemy though isn't the Crypt and I don't  want to spoil it for you but there comes  this point where there's this surprise  and the enemy is really aggressive and  figuring out the secret to killing them  while under pressure was really one of  the high points for me so I'm not gonna  spoil it for you but basically they  chase you and you figure out the secret  to killing them and that feeling of sort  of thinking on your feet that was really  awesome  the main enemy you fight is this  variation of the sunken Knight and  that's one criticism I have of the DLC I  would have liked to see a few different  main enemy designs maybe to break up the  monotony you know there were different  types of knight but every sunken Knight  had the same color palette and the same  sort of model and every single one of  them was dispatched in two swings of my  hammer and killing the same style of  enemy over and over again felt like a  bit of a grind but there is enough  variety to give it interesting for your  first playthrough and I'm not going to  spoil the bosses in a first impressions  video but two out of the three boss  encounters I was really impressed with  both of them have a combination of  unique and familiar design and I was  really happy to see less humanoid and  more monster designs in the DLC one of  the most important things is that they  have this really fully fleshed out array  of attacks that keep you fighting for  your life the entire fight may keep it  unique and they keep it action-packed  it's not like you can just stand back  and heal there's one boss fight that  isn't great and you'll see which one I'm  talking about when you see it but in its  defense it's supposed to be fought in  court  so when you're actually fighting  alongside other phantoms I imagine you  feel like you're fighting your own  battle with the other phantoms you know  you'll be fighting individual enemies  whereas usually when you fight with  phantoms you focus on one enemy and it  feels like you're just ganking them  I feel like this fight will be better in  coop but you'll see what I mean so I  think we should finish this first  impressions by talking about the  presentation and I think I have to start  this by saying that from software you  guys are major teasers they gave  everyone access to the door of the DLC a  week before the DLC actually launched  and a locked door with the whole world  behind it inspires a lot of curiosity as  well as a small bunch of hackers I know  a few people who spend more time trying  to hack into the DLC than actually  playing it which is an adventure through  the game's code not many of us can  comprehend and because they taunted us  with a closed door with the entire DLC  behind it for an entire week one of my  favorite moments was opening that door  and traversing that first crooked  corridor they really did a wonderful job  with that that twisted Hall was such an  awesome distortion of space and the  Vista it opens up to was something to  admire I love it when games foreshadow  your final destination  or your final nemesis and the sunken  ground DLC does both of those things  straight away here are dragons powerful  breathing as you traverse the first hall  and you're also dominated by this  gargantuan pyramid inside a cave which  is really brilliant imagery and also in  terms of presentation the final boss  makes these constant interruptions to  your play all around the map that really  made me feel like this was the Dragons  home you know he's constantly slumbering  wherever he pleases and he made his  presence known and felt by not  particularly caring about the other  inhabitants of the area so the verdict  on Dark Souls two's first package of DLC  is that I think it's easily worth $10  for the fan that enjoyed Dark Souls 2  but in my opinion it's even more worth  it for the fan that didn't enjoy Dark  Souls 2 and you know the DLC can't fix  all the problems with Dark Souls 2 if  you didn't enjoy it but dog Souls 2 is a  different game than the original it  fixes a bunch of issues we all had with  the first game but it also falls short  of a bunch of ambitious ideas the DLC  brings back the awesome level design  we remember from Dark Souls 1 and it  realizes more of what Dark Souls 2 is  capable of it took me about four hours  to complete most of it offline so I  reckon it will take the average player  between three to six hours to complete I  don't know about you but I reckon that's  worth $10 and if the next two pieces of  DLC are as good as this or better then  we're going to end up with a package of  DLC that surpasses what artorias of the  abyss did and it'll be even cheaper if  you want to become a part of my inner  circle here on YouTube then you can  support videos like this one by clicking  on the bonfire and becoming a patron  there's a video on my patreon page which  explains how that works this week is all  about the DLC so look forward to some  more in-depth stuff as well as playing  the game for yourself overall though  guys I hope you got some good  information out of this if you felt like  you had a different first impression  than me then feel free to tell me after  all that is my first impression I feel  like I'm going to change my opinion on  some of this stuff you know when I first  bosses over and over again or when I go  through the level for the umpteenth time  and see how well I remember the level  design so if you had a different opinion  let me know I hope you enjoy the DLC  coverage this week and as usual thank  you for watching my videos you're  awesome and I'll see you soon

